  "body": "**Replaces #433** (opened from the old fork, which can no longer take pushes). Same head commit; nothing else changed.\n\n**Stacked on #432 (#416).** Part of #363.\n\n## What changed\n**With the G76 cycle post option, the G76 words now describe the passes the verdict judged.**\n- The record maps 1:1: `G76 P(m)(r)(a) Q(dmin) R(d)` / `G76 X Z P(k) Q(dd) F`, where:\n  - dd = the first pass's depth;\n  - dmin = the step (constant depth) or the plan's minimum (constant area);\n  - d = the finishing pass's own depth;\n  - m = 1 + spring passes;\n  - a = twice the flank angle.\n- **Control model** (GSK988TA, Fanuc-compatible G76):\n  - each roughing pass goes to `max(dd·√n, previous + dmin)`, up to k − d;\n  - the first finishing pass cuts d, and m − 1 more repeat at k;\n  - each pass starts `depth·tan(a/2)` along Z.\n- **Refused by name with G76 chosen:** alternating infeed (the CL carries a new `alternating` flag; `ALT 1` is written to the stream only when set), a doubled angle outside 0/29/30/55/60/80 (so 29.5° is refused), and a one-pass thread.\n\n**⚠ This changes posted output for the default G76 thread: `R0` becomes `R0.4`.** The golden `fanuc-lathe/thread_g76.nc` changes on one line: `G76 P010000 Q400 R0` → `G76 P010000 Q400 R0.4`.\n- Why: with `R0` the control roughs 0.4, 0.8 and 1.2, then cuts its finishing pass at the root again. That is a fourth full-depth pass the verdict never judged. It's not a gouge, but it isn't what was verified.\n- With `R0.4` it roughs 0.4 and 0.8 and finishes at 1.2: exactly the three passes we judge.\n- Making `R0` true instead would have meant changing G32 output and the judged passes.\n- Note: the #363 research said Q = infeed made the control cut a decreasing ladder. That isn't so, because dmin = infeed clamps every step equal. The real gap was R.\n\n**R comes from the rounded words' own ladder (M3 review fix, head 0bb37ddd).** The control reads the posted words, each rounded to the least increment on its own. In inch, `Q157 P472 R0.0157` left k − d = 0.0315 one increment past the second rung 0.0314, so the control cut a 4th, unverified pass. Now the post counts the record's rough passes N from its own ladder, rebuilds rungs N−1 and N from the rounded Q (first depth), Q (minimum depth) and P (thread height), and writes the R whose last rough depth P − R lies in (rung N−1, rung N], nearest the record's allowance. Inch golden: `R0.0157` → `R0.0158` (control: 0.0157 / 0.0314 / 0.0472). If that R is more than one least increment from the record's allowance, the cycle is refused by name (the Q/Q/P words and the R window are named). The other G76 words (X, Z, P k, Q dd, Q dmin) are each one record value rounded once, not differences, so R was the only derived word. The Tormach (LinuxCNC) G76 has the same class of gap in J/K (inch `J0.0314 K0.0944` = ceil 4 passes for a 3-pass record), filed as #1619. Its goldens are left byte-identical to main here.\n\n**A project cached before this fix posts R0 until the op is regenerated.** Checked: the stale stamp (`crates/app/src/operation/stamp.rs`, `current_stamp`) hashes only the operation's inputs (tool, params, source, heights). It has no generator version, and the cached record's `finish_allowance` reads back as saved. So such an op reads **Fresh**, not Stale, and posts the old `R0` schedule (one extra root pass, mm) until it is regenerated. The stale-stamp machinery does not catch it today.\n\n`docs/decisions/417-g76-matches-verified.md`\n\n## How tested\n- New app `lathe_post_g76_schedule` (4 tests):\n  - a new flank, constant-area golden `thread_g76_area.nc` (`G76 P020055 Q100 R0.05` / `G76 X9.6 Z14 P1200 Q400 F2`): the control's schedule computed from the posted words matches the laid depths and stations within 1 µm;\n  - the default (`R0.4`) and a 0.5 step (`Q500 R0.2`) also match;\n  - one refusal per unexpressible option;\n  - `ALT` survives the stream and the project file.\n- M3 review fix (head 0bb37ddd), two new tests in `lathe_post_g76_schedule`:\n  - `the_default_thread_g76_states_its_three_passes_in_inch`: posts the default thread under G20, parses the G76 words, runs the control's ladder on them, and asserts exactly 3 passes matching the laid depths (`R0.0158`).\n  - `a_ladder_the_rounded_words_cannot_state_is_refused_by_name`: two hand-edited inch records (13 and 39 sqrt rungs, 2 and 4 increments off) are refused by name, and the same record in mm posts.\n  - Mutations, each red and then reverted: R back to `fu(finish)` (inch twin + refusal test); refusal dropped (refusal test); check `> 2` (refusal test, 2-increment case); check `> 0` / `>= 1` (inch twin + refusal test).\n  - Gate: app lathe_post_g76 8, lathe_post_g76_schedule 6, lathe_post_inch 6, lathe_post 7, lathe_post_tormach 8, lathe_post_tormach_thread 8, tormach_lathes 2, recorded_post_streams 5; post lathe_post_refusals 11, script_fanuc_lathe 15; `cargo clippy -p camcraft-app --all-targets --no-deps -- -D warnings` clean. Tormach lathe goldens: `git diff upstream/main` empty.\n- Existing post, cl, inverted_post, lathe_post, golden_cl, project_roundtrip, tool_law, tool_gate and commands_undo suites are green.\n- Takeover refresh at 49d0a3c2: merged upstream/main cleanly, inch golden updated to `R0.0157`; `cargo xtask gate quick --base upstream/main` (escalated to batch) green with 0 failed, incl. clippy -D warnings.\n- `clippy --tests -D warnings` (cl, post, motion, app, ui) is clean.\n- The gate escalated to batch (cl changed). It fails only on tests already red on main: headless GPU, #415, and `swarf_pose_continuity` (PR #351).\n\n## Not exercised / not confirmed by sources (no Hub run needed for the merge)\n1. Which way Fanuc shifts Z per pass for flank infeed. This follows LinuxCNC's sign; either sign is gouge-safe.\n2. Whether the control also shifts each pass's end Z. If not, early passes end up to k·tan θ off at the runout, shallower than the root.\n3. How the minimum-depth clamp applies (read as a per-pass minimum, per the manual's definition).\n4. Rounding of the words: handled now (R from the rounded ladder, refused beyond one increment); individual rungs of a long sqrt ladder can still differ from the laid ones by a few increments, with the same pass count.\n\nNone of these moves the finished thread form.\n\nHub run: not needed. The Tormach lathe goldens are byte-identical to main (checked against upstream/main); only Fanuc-lathe goldens change (`thread_g76.nc` mm and inch, new `thread_g76_area.nc`). The inch Fanuc golden now reads `R0.0158`.\n\nChangelog: G76 threading output states the passes that were verified: the finish allowance R is posted (default threads now post R0.4 instead of R0), and options G76 cannot express are refused by name (#417)\n\nFixes #417\n\n🤖 Generated with [Claude Code](https://claude.com/claude-code)\n\n\n\n",
